<main class="lux-article-page"><nav class="lux-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="<?php echo esc_url(luxureat_static_url('en', '')); ?>">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="<?php echo esc_url(luxureat_static_url('en/blog', '')); ?>">Knowledge Blog</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Caviar Processing</span></nav><article class="lux-reader-layout lux-academy-reader">
    <div class="lux-reader-rule"></div>
    <section class="lux-reader-hero"><div class="lux-reader-hero-copy"><div class="lux-reader-meta-grid"><span>Caviar Academy</span><span>CAVIAR ACADEMY · 2026</span></div><h1 id="lux-reader-title">Caviar Processing</h1><p class="lux-reader-summary">Caviar processing is carried out by hand — from the selection of roe to the Malossol salting method, followed by packaging and maturation.</p></div><figure class="lux-reader-cover"><img width="1600" height="1067" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" src="<?php echo esc_url(get_template_directory_uri() . '/assets/media/academy/caviar-processing-cover.webp'); ?>" alt="Caviar Processing"></figure></section>
    <section class="lux-reader-content"><aside class="lux-reader-aside"><a href="<?php echo esc_url(luxureat_static_url('en/blog', '')); ?>">Back to Knowledge Blog</a></aside><div class="lux-reader-copy"><section class="lux-reader-section lux-reader-section-opening"><p>Sturgeons are processed only when their roe reaches natural maturity, a period that generally falls between the colder months of October and April. To determine the exact moment of maturity, each sturgeon undergoes ultrasound checks.</p><p>The eggs must be harvested when they are not fully mature, retaining good resistance to the osmotic pressure of salt.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-0"><h2>Evisceration</h2><p>Processing is carried out entirely by hand. The ovarian sac is carefully removed, while the sturgeon meat is used to produce fillets or cuts, processed separately.</p><div class="lux-reader-section-media"><figure><img width="768" height="576" loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="<?php echo esc_url(get_template_directory_uri() . '/assets/media/academy/caviar-processing-02.webp'); ?>" alt="Caviar Processing"></figure><figure><img width="918" height="556" loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="<?php echo esc_url(get_template_directory_uri() . '/assets/media/academy/caviar-processing-03.webp'); ?>" alt="Caviar Processing"></figure></div></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-1"><h2>Sieving</h2><p>The roe is gently separated from the ovarian membrane using sieves with the right mesh size to avoid damaging the eggs.</p><div class="lux-reader-section-media"><figure><img width="854" height="590" loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="<?php echo esc_url(get_template_directory_uri() . '/assets/media/academy/caviar-processing-04.webp'); ?>" alt="Caviar Processing"></figure></div></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-2"><h2>Washing and Selection</h2><p>The eggs are then washed with cold water and sorted. Depending on the sturgeon species, the roe is classified by color, size, and flavor.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-3"><h2>Salting</h2><p>Salting is an essential step in caviar preparation. In Europe, all caviars are Malossol (a Russian term meaning “lightly salted”), with about 40 g of salt per kilo of caviar — around 3.5% of the total weight. Salting is done manually with great care. The “Malossol” label is a guarantee of high quality.</p><div class="lux-reader-section-media"><figure><img width="768" height="538" loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="<?php echo esc_url(get_template_directory_uri() . '/assets/media/academy/caviar-processing-05.webp'); ?>" alt="Caviar Processing"></figure></div></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-4"><h2>Packaging</h2><p>After salting, the caviar is placed into original tins of about 1.8 kg, where it matures for 2 to 4 months. During this period, the tins are periodically turned to allow the salt to distribute evenly. Stored at a constant temperature between –2°C and –4°C, caviar in this form can last about 18 months.</p><div class="lux-reader-section-media"><figure><img width="1024" height="827" loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="<?php echo esc_url(get_template_directory_uri() . '/assets/media/academy/main-types-of-caviar-cover.webp'); ?>" alt="Caviar Processing"></figure></div></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-5"><h2>Packaging</h2><p>After salting, the caviar is placed into original tins of about 1.8 kg, where it matures for 2 to 4 months. During this period, the tins are periodically turned to allow the salt to distribute evenly. Stored at a constant temperature between –2°C and –4°C, caviar in this form can last about 18 months.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-6"><h2>Repackaging</h2><p>Subsequently, the caviar is divided into retail tins of 30 g, 50 g, 100 g, 250 g, and 500 g.</p><div class="lux-reader-section-media"><figure><img width="768" height="511" loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="<?php echo esc_url(get_template_directory_uri() . '/assets/media/academy/caviar-processing-07.webp'); ?>" alt="Caviar Processing"></figure></div></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-7"><h2>Caviar Shelf Life</h2><p>Once repackaged, caviar has a shelf life of about 6 months. After opening, it must be consumed within a maximum of 3 days. All preservation times refer to a product kept refrigerated between 0°C and 4°C; higher temperatures significantly reduce its shelf life.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-8"><h2>Can Caviar Be Cooked?</h2><p>Caviar should never be cooked. It must be served with cold or lukewarm dishes to preserve its unique characteristics.</p></section></div></section>
